A type design glyph sheet for extending a Latin typeface into Devanagari. The attached type specimen is the exact style reference: match its stroke weight, low stroke contrast, humanist proportions, terminals and curve tension, as if the same type designer drew a matching Devanagari for this font family. Draw these sixteen Devanagari glyphs in a clean 4 by 4 grid of equal cells on a pure white background, in this order. Row one: अ, क, त, न. Row two: म, र, ल, स. Row three: ष, the conjunct क्ष, the vowel sign ा (aa matra), the vowel sign ि (i matra). Row four: the vowel sign ी (ii matra), the vowel sign े (e matra), the anusvara dot ं, the virama ्. Every letter keeps its headline (shirorekha) at the same height across a row and sits on the same baseline. The vowel signs and marks are drawn alone in their cells, without a base letter and without a dotted circle, at their natural height relative to the headline and baseline. One glyph per cell, centred horizontally. Each glyph is a solid pure black filled shape with clean smooth outlines, no strokes, no gradients, no shading, no texture. No labels, no Latin letters, no guide lines, no borders, no extra marks.
